@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

BrickGame v1.0 (aka Tetris) is a simple console version of the classic Tetris
game implemented in C.
The program is based on a finite state machine (FSM) that manages game states
such as piece spawning, moving, attaching, pausing, and game over.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

To build the game from source, run: @code{make}

@noindent This will produce:

Static library: @code{libtetris.a}

Executable: @code{tetris}


@noindent To install the program system-wide:


@code{sudo make install} 


@noindent By default, the installation prefix is @file{/usr/local}.
You can change it, for example:

@code{make install PREFIX=/home/user/mytetris}

@noindent Installed components:

Executable: @file{/usr/local/bin/tetris}

Library: @file{/usr/local/lib/libtetris.a}

Header files: @file{/usr/local/include/tetris}

@node Gameplay
@chapter Usage, controls and game mechanics

Run the game with:

@example
./tetris
@end example

@noindent Default controls:

@itemize @bullet
@item @kbd{S} -- start new game / restart
@item @kbd{Q} -- quit
@item @kbd{P} -- pause / resume
@item @kbd{LEFT} / @kbd{RIGHT} -- move piece horizontally
@item @kbd{DOWN} -- accelerate piece fall
@item @kbd{R} -- rotate piece clockwise
@end itemize

@noindent Game mechanics

During the game, points are awarded for clearing lines:

@itemize @bullet
@item 1 line — 100 points
@item 2 lines — 300 points
@item 3 lines — 700 points
@item 4 lines — 1500 points
@end itemize

@noindent Every time the player scores @code{600} points, the level increases by 1.
With each new level, the falling speed of the pieces increases.
The maximum number of levels is 10.


@node FSM
@chapter Finite State Machine

The internal logic of the game is modeled as a finite state machine.

@noindent Main states:
@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{START} -- entry point of the program
@item @strong{SPAWN} -- new piece appears
@item @strong{MOVING} -- active piece controlled by user input and timer
@item @strong{SHIFTING} -- automatic downward shift
@item @strong{ATTACHING} -- piece locks in place
@item @strong{FILE_ERROR_STATE} -- error while loading high score file
@item @strong{PAUSE} -- user pauses
@item @strong{GAME_OVER} -- no more space for new pieces
@item @strong{EXIT_STATE} -- program termination
@end itemize

@noindent State transitions triggered by events:
@itemize @bullet
@item Timer expiration -> piece shift
@item Collision detection -> attach piece
@item User input -> move/rotate, pause, quit
@item High score file error -> play without saving results
@item Start or restart -> spawn new piece
@end itemize


@node Implementation
@chapter Implementation

Brief overview of architecture and key components.

@noindent File structure:
@itemize @bullet
@item @file{brick_game/tetris/backend.c, backend.h} -- game state init and management
@item @file{brick_game/tetris/pieces.c, pieces.h} -- tetromino matrices and operations
@item @file{brick_game/tetris/fsm.c, fsm.h} -- finite state machine handlers
@item @file{gui/cli/frontend.c, frontend.h} -- ncurses-based rendering and input
@item @file{gui/cli/tetris.c} -- main entry point and game loop
@item @file{brick_game/tetris/types.h} -- main data types and constants
@item @file{brick_game/tetris/helpers.c, helpers.h} -- high score handling
@item @file{tests/*} -- unit tests for FSM and pieces
@end itemize

@node Uninstallation
@chapter Uninstallation

There are two ways to remove components, depending on what you want to clean up.

@noindent To remove installed system-wide components run:

@example
sudo make uninstall
@end example

@noindent This will delete:
@itemize @bullet
@item @code{tetris} executable from @file{$(PREFIX)/bin}
@item @code{libtetris.a} library from @file{$(PREFIX)/lib}
@item Header files from @file{$(PREFIX)/include/tetris}
@end itemize

@noindent To clean only local build artifacts (without touching installed files), run:

@example
make clean
@end example

@noindent This will delete:
@itemize @bullet
@item Build directory @file{build/} with object files
@item Local binaries @code{tetris} and @code{test_app}
@item Static library @code{libtetris.a} from the project root
@item Coverage reports in @file{coverage/}
@end itemize

@node Distribution
@chapter Distribution

To create a release archive:

@example
make dist
@end example

@noindent This generates @file{tetris-1.0.tar.gz} with source files, headers, tests, and Makefile.


@node MakefileTargets
@chapter Makefile Targets

Build targets from @file{Makefile}:


@noindent @code{all}
Build executable and static library.
@example
make all
@end example

@noindent @code{re}
Full rebuild (clean + all).
@example
make re
@end example

@noindent @code{test_build}
Build tests only.
@example
make test_build
@end example

@noindent @code{test}
Build and run tests.
@example
make test
@end example

@noindent @code{coverage_build}
Build with coverage flags.
@example
make coverage_build
@end example

@noindent @code{gcov_report}
Generate coverage report in @file{coverage} directory.
@example
make gcov_report
@end example

@noindent @code{clean}
Remove build artifacts, binaries, libraries, coverage reports.
@example
make clean
@end example

@noindent @code{install}
Install binaries, libraries, headers.
@example
sudo make install
@end example

@noindent @code{uninstall}
Remove installed files.
@example
sudo make uninstall
@end example

@noindent @code{dist}
Create source distribution archive @file{tetris-1.0.tar.gz}.
@example
make dist
@end example

@noindent @code{info} / @code{pdf} / @code{dvi}
Build documentation (Info / PDF / DVI).
@example
make info
make pdf
make dvi
@end example

@noindent @code{doc-clean}
Clean documentation artifacts (@file{.info}, @file{.pdf}, @file{.dvi}).
@example
make doc-clean
@end example

@noindent @code{cppcheck}
Run static code analysis with cppcheck.
@example
make cppcheck
@end example

@noindent @code{valgrind}
Run tests under Valgrind to check for memory leaks.
@example
make valgrind
@end example

@noindent @code{format}
Check source formatting with clang-format (Google style).
@example
make format
@end example


